Usages:
  provider_registry_pattern: |
    lib/promscrape/discovery/ has ~22 sibling provider packages (azure, consul, consulagent,
    digitalocean, dns, docker, dockerswarm, ec2, eureka, gce, hetzner, http, kubernetes, kuma,
    linode, marathon, nomad, openstack, ovhcloud, puppetdb, vultr, yandexcloud). Each exposes an
    SDConfig type embedded as a []SDConfig slice field directly on lib/promscrape's
    ScrapeConfig struct (one field per provider, e.g. ConsulSDConfigs, EC2SDConfigs,
    GCESDConfigs) — there is no reflective/dynamic registry, just static struct composition.
    Almost every provider's SDConfig implements a shared poll-on-demand shape,
    GetLabels(baseDir string) ([]Labels, error), dispatched uniformly by lib/promscrape's
    getScrapeWorkGeneric helper. This cell (kubernetes) is the exception: instead of one-shot
    polling, it runs a persistent watch connection, so it exposes MustStart/GetScrapeWorkObjects/
    MustStop instead of a plain GetLabels. Confirmed directly on two other providers: consul's
    and gce's SDConfig both implement "GetLabels(baseDir string) ([]Labels, error)" and
    "MustStop()", nothing else.

Annotations: |
  This cell is one of ~22 sibling packages under lib/promscrape/discovery/ that together form
  VictoriaMetrics's service-discovery extension-point registry — see `provider_registry_pattern`
  for how a new provider plugs in and why kubernetes's shape differs from most siblings.
  Kubernetes is the largest of the 21 non-native providers (2,914 lines, ~23% of the whole
  discovery tree) because it runs a stateful long-poll watcher against the Kubernetes API server
  and incrementally recomputes scrape targets from an in-memory object cache, rather than
  re-polling from scratch on every refresh interval like most siblings.

---

"SDConfig(apiServer: string, role: string, kubeConfigFile: string)":
  location: kubernetes.go
  annotations: |
    User-facing YAML config schema for kubernetes_sd_configs.

    `role`: one of node, pod, service, endpoints, endpointslice, ingress — determines which
    Kubernetes API objects are watched and how target labels are derived from them
    `apiServer`: explicit Kubernetes API server URL; when empty, in-cluster service-account
    discovery is used instead
    `kubeConfigFile`: alternative to `apiServer`/in-cluster auth — read credentials from a
    kubeconfig file

    Use `provider_registry_pattern` to see how this differs from most sibling providers.
  methods:
    "MustStart(baseDir: string, swcFunc: ScrapeWorkConstructorFunc)": |
      Resolve auth (in-cluster token, kubeConfigFile, or explicit config), open a persistent
      watch connection to the Kubernetes API server for the configured role, and begin
      incrementally maintaining a cache of discovered objects.

      `baseDir`: directory used to resolve relative file paths referenced in the config
      `swcFunc`: called per discovered/changed object's derived labels to build a caller-defined
      scrape-work representation
    "GetScrapeWorkObjects() -> objects:[]Any, err:error": |
      Return the currently cached set of scrape-work objects built from watched Kubernetes
      objects; non-blocking, always reflects the latest watch state. `err` reports a startup
      failure recorded when the watch connection was opened (e.g. an auth or connectivity
      error), if one occurred.
    "MustStop()": |
      Tear down the watch connection and release cached state.

"ScrapeWorkConstructorFunc(metaLabels: Labels) -> workObject:Any":
  location: kubernetes.go
  annotations: |
    Caller-supplied hook, given to `SDConfig`'s own MustStart method, that turns one discovered
    object's meta-labels (__meta_kubernetes_* labels) into whatever scrape-work representation
    the caller (lib/promscrape) needs.

